# ── npm-workspace: Node/JS-ecosystem artifacts the VisualStudio rules don't cover ──
# The workspace re-inclusion at the TOP of this file lets every VisualStudio rule
# re-ignore VS build artifacts under /packages. These categories have NO VisualStudio
# global rule, so they are scoped-ignored here (at the END, guaranteed last word) so
# a secret or generated artifact can never be committed under the workspace.
/packages/**/node_modules/
/packages/**/.npm/
/packages/**/.npmrc
/packages/**/.cache/
/packages/**/.nyc_output/
/packages/**/coverage/
/packages/**/coverage*.json
/packages/**/coverage*.xml
/packages/**/coverage*.info
/packages/**/*.lcov
/packages/**/.env
/packages/**/.env.*
/packages/**/*.pfx
/packages/**/*.snk
/packages/**/*.pem
/packages/**/*.key
/packages/**/secrets.json
/packages/**/[Bb]in/
/packages/**/[Oo]ut/

# ── npm-workspace: re-include the SOURCE directory literally named `release` ──
# The shared release-protection evaluator lives at `packages/core/src/release/`
# (TypeScript source, NOT build output). The VisualStudio `[Rr]elease/` build-results
# rule far above matches ANY directory named `release`/`Release` at any depth, so it
# would ignore this source directory (git will not descend into an ignored directory,
# which would silently drop `tagRulesetEval.ts` from commits despite it being imported
# and inventoried). Re-include that specific workspace SOURCE directory as the last
# word. This is scoped to the one known source path, so genuine VS build output named
# `release/` elsewhere in the workspace is still ignored by the rule above.
!/packages/core/src/release/